# Site stack

**Static HTML + CSS. No JS framework. No build step required for v1.**

## Do

- Hand-written pages a human can edit in a weekend
- `css/tokens.css` (palette) + `css/site.css` (layout/type)
- Pretty URLs via directories with `index.html` (`/support`, `/privacy`)
- Images in `images/` with boring names (WebP + JPEG fallback)
- GitHub Pages from `main`, site at repo root
- Local preview: `python3 -m http.server` in the repo root
- Progressive enhancement: the site works with JavaScript disabled

## Do not

- Next.js, React, Tailwind-in-JS, a CMS, Docker, Netlify plugins, serverless
- CSS-in-JS or utility soup that hides the design
- Client-side routing
- Eleventy / npm in v1 unless writing HTML by hand is already painful
- Publish `resources/` as the live image tree (that folder is source art)

## JavaScript

Zero JS on `/privacy` (and later `/terms`). Other pages may use up to about
150 lines of vanilla JS each (`BLUEPRINT.md` §11.2): `defer`, no libraries,
never needed to render or read content. Core interactions (the Stage,
appearance switch, tabs, chapter links) are CSS-first and work with JS off.
No analytics, font-loading observers, cookie scripts, or storage APIs
(`localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB).

Stable URLs: `/`, `/support`, `/privacy`, plus `/features` and `/history`.
Never reorganize the first three without redirects.
